# Milestone: v0.10.2-reactive (W1)
# Covers: nested `every (P1) { every (P2) X }` — outer periodic whose body
#         installs an inner periodic each fire.
## timeout: 180
# Timeout rationale: each 1us outer fire spawns a FRESH inner UPeriodic,
# so the periodics list grows per pump; under URBI_GC_STRESS
# (collect-per-alloc with O(N) sidecar scans) the workload is
# quadratic-plus and blew the default 30s on a contended 4-vCPU CI
# runner while staying <1s locally.  The accumulation semantic itself
# is the documented v1.x question below.
#
# Activated 2026-05-25 (W1): OP_CLOSURE in body strands fixed (reactive F4).
# Prior to the fix: body strand had root_proto == NULL so OP_CLOSURE at
# frame_count==0 could not find executing_proto->nested[] and halted with
# "CLOSURE: proto index out of range".
#
# What this fixture asserts:
#   - The source parses and installs without error.
#   - The outer every() body fires (no OP_CLOSURE error produced).
#   - The inner every() installs correctly from the outer body (inner_count
#     stays 0 because the inner period is 1d; the test runs in milliseconds).
#
# Note on accumulation semantics: each outer-body fire spawns a FRESH inner
# UPeriodic.  The inner period is 1d so no inner fires occur during the
# test window.  The accumulation semantic is a v1.x question; deferred to
# when tag-scoped inner periodics land.
#
# Note: sleep(duration) not yet available (W6 pending); the trailing
# no-op lines drive the cooperative scheduler instead.

var inner_count = 0
every (1us) { every (1d) Realm.inner_count = Realm.inner_count + 1 }
0
0
Realm.inner_count
[00000000] 0
[00000000] nil
[00000000] 0
[00000000] 0
[00000000] 0
